{% set local_problem %}
<p>Sand Mountain's clay soil creates drainage challenges. Water doesn't soak in — it runs off and pools. When surface downspout extensions deposit water just a few feet from your foundation, that water often finds its way right back to your house.</p>

<p>Some Albertville properties have it worse: sloped lots where water naturally flows toward the house, low spots that collect runoff, or areas where the water table is high. For these situations, surface solutions aren't enough.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve the problem by routing water through buried pipes to a discharge point far from your home. No more pooling, no more seepage, no more worry every time it rains hard.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water away through a sloped channel. The water exits at a predetermined point — property edge, drainage area, or dry well — far from your foundation.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or PVC pipe buried 8-12 inches deep. The system is designed with proper slope so water flows by gravity — no pumps, no electricity, no maintenance. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release the water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>It's a permanent, set-it-and-forget-it solution for properties where surface drainage isn't enough.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage systems throughout Marshall County for over 20 years. We understand Sand Mountain's soil conditions and how to design systems that actually solve problems, not just move them around.</p>

<p>Underground drains are an investment. We'll be honest about whether your situation really needs them or if a simpler solution will work.</p>
{% endset %}
